 ## Overview
-Octree-based out-of-core volume rendering is a method for rendering multi-channel volume datasets that exceed available texture memory by storing octree nodes and bricks on disk and loading them on demand [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It extends the single-channel approach of Crassin et al. to support multiple visible channels by storing pointers and culling metadata in each node [S1, unofficial]. Unlike the [Residency Octree](5269-residency-octree.md), which uses an octree for hierarchical culling but allows partial subtree residency, this method enforces that the whole subtree is resident in the cache for high-resolution rendering, leading to higher memory footprint and worse performance for multiple channels compared to the author's page-table method [S1, unofficial].
+Octree-based out-of-core volume rendering is a method for rendering multi-channel volume datasets that exceed available texture memory by storing octree nodes and bricks on disk and loading them on demand [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It extends the single-channel approach of Crassin et al. to support multiple visible channels by storing pointers and culling metadata in each node [S1, unofficial]. Unlike the [Residency Octree](5269-residency-octree.md), which uses an octree for hierarchical culling but allows partial subtree residency, this method enforces that the whole subtree is resident in the cache for high-resolution rendering, leading to a higher memory footprint and worse performance for multiple channels compared to the author's page-table method [S1, unofficial].
 
